title: SleepyRunner Generalized

files:
  - .assets/img-104-03-01.png
  - em.mark:em.mark/ActivityI.em.ts
  - em.mark:em.mark.busywait/BusyWaitA.em.ts
  - em.mark:em.mark/RunnerP.em.ts
  - em.mark:em.mark.busywait/BusyWaitC.em.ts
  - em.mark:em.mark.coremark/CoreMarkA.em.ts
  - .assets/img-104-03-02.png

steps:
  - cmds:
      - view 1
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;%[cx]em.mark.*% Architecture}

      Our generalization of {[cu]SleepyRunnerP} organizes bench&shy;mark elements across a family of `em.mark.*` buckets.

      The {[cu]RunnerP} program {[dc,1]} anchors this architecture by calling {[cu]ActivityI} delegates {[dc,2]} at run-time.

      <div class="em-info">

      Each conformant **Delegate** implements the actual work&shy;load defined by a particular benchmark.

      </div>

      A corresponding set of EMS **Composites** {[dc,3]} orchestrate _build-time_ configuration and binding of {[dc,1]} and {[dc,2]}.

      This tour first examines these elements, then sets the stage for defining additional benchmark activities.

  - cmds:
      - open 2
    text: |
      {[ht,$peek]&thinsp;%[cu]ActivityI% &ndash; Quick Look}

      The {[cu]ActivityI} **Interface** (found in the `em.mark` **Bucket**) defines the run-time behavior of benchmark workloads.

      Besides implementing the specified functions, a conform&shy;ing **Delegate** often exports additional config parameters.

      <div class="em-info">

      Depending upon the {[cu]ActivityI} implementation, func&shy;tions other than {[cf]exec} may remain empty.

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - open 3
      - mark 3 A 2
      - mark 3 B 7
      - mark 3 C 12
    focus: [3, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]BusyWaitA% &ndash; Details}

      The {[cu]BusyWaitA} activity provides a rudi&shy;mentary spring&shy;board for implementing more complex benchmarks.

      {[BM,A]} {[cu]BusyWaitA} declares its conformance to {[cu]ActivityI}<br>
      {[BM,B]} {[cn]duration_us} configures the workload's length<br>
      {[BM,C]} {[cf]exec} invokes the current distro's {[cn]BusyWait} proxy<br>

      <div class="em-info">

      Note that {[cu]BusyWaitA} provides empty implementations of {[cf]setup}, {[cf]report}, and {[cf]teardown}.

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - open 4
      - mark 4 A 9
      - mark 4 B 11
      - mark 4 C 12
      - mark 4 D 14
      - mark 4 E 15
      - mark 4 F 21
    focus: [4, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]RunnerP% &ndash; Build-time}

      In generalizing {[cu]SleepyRunnerP}&thinsp;{[tr,102/07/04]}&thinsp;, {[cu]RunnerP} ex&shy;ports additional features for clients to bind at build-time:

      {[BM,A]} the conforming benchmark workload<br>
      {[BM,B]} the duration of the WORK-SLEEP event interval<br>
      {[BM,C]} the number of WORK-SLEEP cycles executed<br>
      {[BM,D]} override the {[cs]'em.build.Optimize'} build property<br>
      {[BM,E]} override the {[cs]'em.build.BootFlash'} build property<br>

      {[cs]em$configure} tests the latter two configs {[bm,F]} and over&shy;rides their corresponding property using an intrinsic setter.

  - cmds:
      - open 4
      - mark 4 A 9
      - mark 4 B 11
      - mark 4 C 12
      - mark 4 D 14
      - mark 4 E 15
      - mark 4 F 21
      - mark 4 G 34
      - mark 4 H 41
      - mark 4 I 43
      - mark 4 J 46

    focus: [4, 21]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]RunnerP% &ndash; Run-time}

      As in {[cu]SleepyRunnerP}&thinsp;{[tr,102/07/04]}, a private {[cn]ticker} ob&shy;ject and {[cn]count} variable pace the run-time execution.

      <div class="em-info">

      Note how the {[cn]wake_count} and {[cn]interval_ms} configs replace the manifest constants used in {[cu]SleepyRunnerP}.

      </div>

      Invoking the {[cn]Activity} _life-cycle_ methods at {[bm,G]}&thinsp;&ndash;{[bm,J]} tracks the control-flow used within {[cu]SleepyRunnerP}.

      Inside {[cf]tickCB}, {[bm,H]} executes a _single_ burst of benchmark activity before reporting {[bm,I]} any interim results.

      <div class="em-info">

      As in {[cu]SleepyRunnerP}, toggling {[cd]%%d} around {[bm,H]} allows us to meas&shy;ure activity execution time using a logic analyzer.

      </div>

      Once {[cn]count} decrements to `0`, {[bm,J]} performs benchmark finalization before halting {[cu]RunnerP}.

  - cmds:
      - open 5
      - mark 5 A 9
      - mark 5 B 10
    focus: [5, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]BusyWaitC% &ndash; Details}

      Though {[cu]RunnerP} furnishes the {[cs]em$run} implementation, activity-specific **Composites** drive the program build.

      In the case of {[cu]BusyWaitC} this entails:

      {[BM,A]} including {[cu]RunnerP} in the target image via {[cs]$using}<br>
      {[BM,B]} delegating the {[cn]Activity} proxy to {[cu]BusyWaitA}<br>

      <div class="em-info">

      Our {[cs]em$configure} meta-function follows the same design pattern found in a typical distro {[cu]BoardC}.

      </div>

      Though not seen here, {[cu]BusyWaitC} could explicitly over&shy;ride config params exported by {[cu]RunnerP} and {[cu]BusyWaitA}.

      <div class="em-info">

      As activities and config choices multiply, how should we manage a _family_ of benchmark **Composites**?

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - open 6
    text: |
      {[ht,$peek]&thinsp;%[cu]CoreMarkA% &ndash; Quick Look}

      With the workload encapsulated by {[cu]CoreBench} {[tr,102/02/05]}, implementation of {[cu]CoreMarkA} proves trivial.

      <div class="em-info">

      The corresponding {[cu]CoreMarkC} **Composite** proves just as straightforward &ndash; cloning the design of {[cu]BusyWaitC}.

      </div>

      To mimic {[cu]SleepyRunnerP}, this benchmark activity uses its {[cf]report} function to print each checksum.

  - cmds:
      - view 1
    text: |
      {[ht,$extra]&thinsp;Extra Credit}

      {[bi,task_alt]} Vary {[cu]BusyWaitC} duration and cycle count.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Optimize {[cu]CoreMarkC} for speed; compare code size.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Implement {[cu]PauseA} with light-sleep, not busy-wait.

      &nbsp;

      <div class="em-info">

      How do these changes affect energy efficiency&thinsp;??<br>
      Our next tour will let you put them to the test&thinsp;!!

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - view 7
    text: |
      {[ht,$steps]&thinsp;Next Steps}

      The `em.mark.*` framework has three axes of variability:

      {[DC,1]} {[cu]RunnerP}, with its proxy and config parameters<br>
      {[DC,2]} {[cu]ActivityI} implementations, with their own configs<br>
      {[DC,3]} benchmark **Composites**, which glue it all together<br>

      Our final tour circles back to **EM&bull;Scope**, where we'll learn how to _grab&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;scan&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;view_ in your own capture folder&thinsp;{[dc,4]}.

      {[hc]}
